XDJ-RX patch 16: pace SDMA audio periods like the hardware (fixes audio stutter).

The guest's ALSA ring is 2 periods x 64 frames (2.9 ms). Patch 11 completed
periods from the host audio callback, which QEMU only runs every few ms (measured
~345 calls/s on macOS vs 689 periods/s needed), so each call completed ~2 periods
-- the whole ring -- at once. ALSA's hw_ptr then jumped a full buffer, avail hit
the stop threshold and rbp logged "ALSA: Resolve xrun": audible dropouts that got
worse with load (two decks + FX + tempo: 378 xruns in 30 s).

Now a pacer thread completes at most ONE period per channel per tick, evenly
spaced (sleeping to absolute deadlines on its own thread, because the main loop
can't wake every 1.45 ms on macOS: no ppoll, ms-granular poll() timeouts), and
copies the master channel's period into a PCM FIFO that the host callback drains.
The tick interval is trimmed +/-8% by the FIFO level, locking the guest period
clock (and the playhead) to the host audio clock. A wake-up more than a period
late resyncs instead of catching up in a burst. Same stress test: 2 xruns / 30 s,
pacer at 689 periods/s (= 44.1 kHz), no host underruns.
  XDJ_AUDIO_LATENCY_MS  FIFO target, default 20 (3..300)
  XDJ_AUDIO_STATS=1     every 5 s: host underruns/gap, cushion, FIFO level,
                        guest-late periods, pacer rate/resyncs, rate trim
The no-voice fallback (per-channel QEMU timers) is unchanged.

